{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1271"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c106): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c106.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1271"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge everyday/food","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/everyday__food.g3.json","changes":[{"field":"titleEn","old":"Peas, Onions and Potatoes: Vegetables That Fed Civilisations","new":"Peas, Rice and Potatoes: The Crops That Fed Civilisations"},{"field":"titleDe","old":"Erbsen, Zwiebeln und Kartoffeln: Gemüse, das Zivilisationen ernährte","new":"Erbsen, Reis und Kartoffeln: Pflanzen, die Zivilisationen ernährten"},{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",254],["+","Rice took a parallel path in East Asia. Probably domesticated rice was already in use around 7000 BC in China's Lijiacun culture on the Yangtze, and from paddy fields there it spread across southern and south-east Asia; today it is the staple food of more than half of the world's population. Rye, by contrast, began as a weed. The earliest grains, found at Tell Abu Hureyra in northern Syria, date to around 6600 BC, but it travelled north among the wheat and barley and came into its own only where they struggled – on the poor, cold soils of northern and eastern Europe. It contains gluten, so it is naturally not gluten-free, and its coarse meal gives Westphalian pumpernickel, baked for up to a day, its dark colour.\n\n"],["=",515],["+"," Maize had made the same crossing. Domesticated from wild teosinte in southern Mexico some 9,000 years ago, it was the grain of the Americas long before Columbus: the Muisca of present-day Colombia were already making arepas from it about 3,000 years ago, and these filled dough patties, today made from pre-cooked flour of maize (corn), remain the daily bread of Colombia and Venezuela."],["=",175],["-","Vegetable"],["+","Crop"],["=",116],["+","\n| rice | Yangtze, China | c. 7000 BC |\n| rye | Near East, as a weed | northern and eastern Europe |\n| maize | southern Mexico | the Americas, c. 7000 BC onwards |"]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",246],["+","Reis nahm in Ostasien einen parallelen Weg. Vermutlich domestizierter Reis wurde schon um 7000 v. Chr. in der chinesischen Lijiacun-Kultur am Jangtsekiang genutzt, und von den Nassfeldern dort verbreitete er sich über Süd- und Südostasien; heute ist er das Grundnahrungsmittel von mehr als der Hälfte der Weltbevölkerung. Roggen dagegen begann als Unkraut. Die ältesten Körner, gefunden in Tell Abu Hureyra im Norden Syriens, stammen aus der Zeit um 6600 v. Chr., doch er wanderte zwischen Weizen und Gerste nach Norden und kam erst dort zu Ehren, wo diese sich schwertaten – auf den kargen, kalten Böden Nord- und Osteuropas. Er enthält Gluten, ist also von Natur aus nicht glutenfrei, und sein Schrot gibt dem westfälischen Pumpernickel, der bis zu einem Tag lang gebacken wird, seine dunkle Farbe.\n\n"],["=",4],["-","hatten"],["+","legten"],["=",7],["-","Reisen."],["+","Wege"],["=",1],["+","zurück. "],["=",501],["+"," Mais hatte dieselbe Überfahrt gemacht. Vor rund 9.000 Jahren im Süden Mexikos aus der Wildform Teosinte domestiziert, war er lange vor Kolumbus das Getreide Amerikas: Die Muisca im heutigen Kolumbien backten schon vor etwa 3.000 Jahren Arepas daraus, und diese gefüllten Teigfladen, heute aus vorgekochtem Maismehl, sind bis heute das tägliche Brot Kolumbiens und Venezuelas."],["=",161],["-","Gemüse"],["+","Pflanze"],["=",120],["+","\n| Reis | Jangtsekiang, China | um 7000 v. Chr. |\n| Roggen | Vorderasien, als Unkraut | Nord- und Osteuropa |\n| Mais | Südmexiko | Amerika, ab etwa 7000 v. Chr. |"]]},{"field":"notesEn","diff":[["=",67],["+"," Arepas are mostly made from pre-cooked maize flour; wheat-flour versions exist in the Andes."]]},{"field":"notesDe","diff":[["=",57],["+"," Arepas werden meist aus vorgekochtem Maismehl gemacht; in den Anden gibt es auch Varianten aus Weizenmehl."]]}]},
